<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  launchd user agent for agent-terminald.
  @TEMPLATE_NOTE_BEGIN@
  THIS IS A TEMPLATE, and everything down to the END marker is dropped from the
  rendered copy — including this paragraph, so that the installed file never
  claims to be something you still have to edit. `make install` substitutes the
  placeholders and writes the usable copy to PREFIX/share/agent-terminal/. Copy
  THAT file, not this one:

    make install PREFIX="$HOME/.local"
    cp "$HOME/.local/share/agent-terminal/dev.agentterminal.daemon.plist" \
       ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.agentterminal.daemon.plist

  It is a template because the path had to be wrong for somebody. A shipped
  plist naming /usr/local/bin is wrong for everyone who followed AGENTS.md and
  installed to ~/.local, and the way it is wrong is the bad way: if an older
  /usr/local/bin/agent-terminald is still there from a previous install, launchd
  starts THAT one, it answers the socket first, and the client's sibling-first
  autospawn never runs because a daemon is already answering. The protocol skips
  frames it predates, so the new client goes quiet rather than erroring — a
  stale daemon is an unpatched daemon, and it looks like a feature that does
  nothing. Unsubstituted, this file fails loudly instead, because launchd cannot
  exec a path that still has the placeholder in it.
  @TEMPLATE_NOTE_END@

  Upgrade the binary without killing your sessions:
    launchctl kill SIGHUP gui/$(id -u)/dev.agentterminal.daemon
  or equivalently, and preferred because it does not need the label:
    agent-terminal reload

  `reload` re-execs the daemon in place, so the pid never changes and no PTY
  master fd is ever closed — no child sees a SIGHUP. Measured against launchd
  itself, not assumed: after `launchctl kill SIGHUP` the job's reported PID is
  unchanged, LastExitStatus stays 0, and launchd keeps supervising the same
  process. `launchctl kickstart -k` does NOT do this — it SIGKILLs the job and
  starts a new one, which closes every master fd and hangs up every child.

  Notes:
  - Runs in the foreground (-f); launchd owns the lifecycle.
  - Unlike systemd, launchd needs no KillMode equivalent. Measured: on
    `launchctl bootout`, only the main process is signalled — a setsid
    grandchild keeps running and reparents to pid 1. So PTY children survive
    an unload here, whereas systemd's default KillMode=control-group would
    kill them (see contrib/systemd/agent-terminald.service.in).
  - PATH: launchd gives agents only /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin, and the
    daemon's children inherit it. A session command that lives anywhere else
    (~/.local/bin, /opt/homebrew/bin) then dies instantly with exec 127 —
    the session appears to "do nothing". The EnvironmentVariables block
    below ships the common locations plus your install prefix; add yours.
    ($HOME does not expand in plists — which is the other reason this is a
    template: an absolute path is the only thing launchd accepts here, so it
    cannot be written once for every reader.)
  - For SSH sessions to inherit your agent, launchd must know
    SSH_AUTH_SOCK. On macOS the system launchd sets it for user agents by
    default; if you use a custom agent path, add it to EnvironmentVariables.
  - ProgramArguments[0] needs no adjusting: it is your PREFIX/bin, filled in at
    install time. If you move the binary afterwards, re-run `make install` with
    the new PREFIX and copy the regenerated file again.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>dev.agentterminal.daemon</string>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>@UNIT_PATH@</string>
    </dict>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>@BINDIR@/agent-terminald</string>
        <string>-f</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Matches systemd's Restart=on-failure. A bare `KeepAlive true`
         respawns even after a clean exit, and SIGTERM *is* a clean exit here
         (main.c calls loop_quit and returns 0), so a deliberate `kill` of the
         daemon came straight back — and the two shipped units disagreed about
         what stopping the daemon means.

         Measured rather than read off the manual: with SuccessfulExit=false,
         a job exiting 3 was started 3 times in 26 s — launchd throttles
         respawns to roughly 10 s — while a job exiting 0 was started exactly
         once and left alone. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <dict>
        <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
        <false/>
    </dict>

    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>

    <!-- The daemon logs to stderr. Not a private PrivateTmp as on systemd:
         this really is the shared /tmp, so the path is predictable and
         world-readable by default. Nothing secret is logged, but session
         names appear here. -->
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/agent-terminald.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
